The code statements that lead to the bug are:
struct vlan_hdr *vlan = (struct vlan_hdr *)skb->data;
protocol = vlan->h_vlan_encapsulated_proto;
skb_pull(skb, VLAN_HLEN);
Reason:
These statements directly use skb->data to read and pull VLAN headers without first verifying that the full VLAN header (VLAN_HLEN bytes) is present in the linear area. If only part of the header is linearized, the read of `h_vlan_encapsulated_proto` accesses memory beyond the linear area, causing out-of-bounds reads. The subsequent `skb_pull` may also violate skb invariants (e.g., pulling beyond available linear data).

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (!pskb_may_pull(skb, VLAN_HLEN))
    goto drop;
vlan = (struct vlan_hdr *)skb->data;
protocol = vlan->h_vlan_encapsulated_proto;
skb_pull(skb, VLAN_HLEN);
Reason:
The added `pskb_may_pull(skb, VLAN_HLEN)` ensures that the entire VLAN header is pulled into the linear area before accessing it. If the header is not fully linear, the function drops the packet via the existing error path (`goto drop`). This prevents out-of-bounds reads and maintains skb invariants.

